{
  "image_name": "0d3906ccb0a245b64339ec2befb2f178.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes two ways to therapeutically target oncogenic fusion proteins, using PML–RARα as the main example. In the upper part, this fusion protein sits on DNA at a response element and recruits co-repressors such as HDAC, DNMT, and PRC2, keeping chromatin in a repressed state and blocking expression of genes needed for granulocytic differentiation. Treatment reverses this block in different ways. ATRA promotes a more active chromatin state with co-activators and histone acetylation; at lower concentrations it appears to switch PML–RARα cofactors, and at higher concentrations it can drive degradation of the fusion protein. Arsenic trioxide also leads to PML–RARα degradation, though with more limited transcriptional changes. Both routes ultimately restore granulocytic differentiation.\n\nThe lower part presents a broader strategy for fusion protein targeting with bifunctional degrader compounds. A small molecule binds the fusion protein and simultaneously recruits a ubiquitin ligase complex, causing ubiquitination and bringing the target into contact with the proteasome. The result is selective degradation of the fusion protein into fragments. Overall, the figure contrasts transcriptional reprogramming and direct protein destruction as complementary approaches to neutralize oncogenic fusion drivers.",
